  <div class="num">08</div>
  <h2>Where this is heading</h2>
  <p class="lede">Two things worth knowing about before you get too settled.</p>
  <h3>A new kind of model that does not chat</h3>
  <p>
    There is a new kind of AI that does not chat at all. It makes decisions. It
    is called <a
      href="https://typesafe.ai/blog/introducing-system-one-models-and-jev"
      >Jev</a
    >. You hand it a situation and a list of possible decisions, it picks one
    and tells you how sure it is. Because it is not writing paragraphs, it is
    dramatically faster and cheaper than the models you have used so far.
  </p>
  <p>
    I have been testing it for work and it is legit. To get a feel for what
    people are doing with it, have a look at <a href="https://jevable.com/"
      >this gallery of projects built with Jev</a
    >. One builder used it to make an agent that finds flights in about seven
    seconds for less than half a cent. Another <a
      href="https://dev.to/trknhr/jev-x-highwayenv-60-seconds-without-a-crash-30ig"
      >taught a car to drive itself in a driving game</a
    > and it went a full minute on the highway without a crash.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why it matters: a lot of work is small decisions made over and over. Is this
    message urgent? Is this photo worth keeping? Asking a top model every one of
    those questions has been too slow or too expensive. A job that costs a
    hundred dollars on a top model costs well under a dollar on Jev. At that
    price a lot more is worth automating. Once you have a site running, ask
    yourself what you sort by hand all day, and test it there.
  </p>
  <p>
    I also put out my first YouTube video about Jev, <a
      href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6FT9GpKaaYQ">here</a
    >. I did it on a whim so the quality is low, but it goes deeper than this
    page does.
  </p>
  <h3>If it goes well</h3>
  <p>
    After all the warnings, it is worth reading something hopeful. The price of
    using AI at the original ChatGPT's level fell by more than 280 times between
    late 2022 and late 2024. If that keeps going, things like personalised
    medicine and a world class tutor for every kid end up within reach of
    ordinary people, not only companies. Matt Shumer puts the stakes better than
    I can: "I think a world where someone you love finds a lump, and you still
    sleep that night, is really on the table." The small version of that for you
    is this: the tools in this guide are the same ones the professionals use, at
    the same price. There is no upgraded version being kept from you.
  </p>
  <p style="font-size: 15px; color: var(--color-4f9a68)">
    Read: <a href="https://somethingbig.ai/what-if-ai-goes-well"
      >What if AI goes well</a
    > and <a href="https://somethingbig.ai/skills-upgrade">Skills upgrade</a>.
  </p>
